1
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:55,000
On September 19, 1957, executives and scientists of the National Reactor Testing Station in

2
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,000
in Idaho assembled in a control room to witness the birth of a powerful new nuclear test reactor,

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:09,000
the largest known facility of its type. Its name, the Engineering Test Reactor, or ETR.

4
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:14,000
It is a major development in the United States Atomic Energy Commission's program to advance

5
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,000
construction of economic nuclear reactors for civilian and industrial use and to convert

6
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:24,000
aircraft and ships to nuclear propulsion. The reactor is being used principally to determine

7
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,000
the effects of intense neutron and gamma ray bombardment in large-scale testing of fuel

8
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:36,000
elements and reactor components under conditions to be met in nuclear power plants of the future.

9
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Two outstanding features of the ETR design are bottom positioning of the control rods,

10
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
leaving the top of the reactor free for experimental access and providing large experimental spaces

11
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,000
within the heart of the core.

12
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,000
The ETR is water-cooled and moderated with U-235 enriched fuel, both beryllium and aluminum

13
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:03,000
as reflectors, and has a power rating of 175 megawatts.

14
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:09,000
It will produce heat fluxes ranging from 440,000 BTU per hour per square foot to a maximum

15
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
of 1,150,000 BTU.

16
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,000
It will provide an average thermal flux of 4 times 10 to the 14th power, and an average

17
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:25,000
fast neutron flux of 1.5 times 10 to the 15th. These conditions will be equal to, or in many

18
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:30,000
cases exceed, those likely to be found in any reactor for which components are to be tested.

19
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:38,000
The test reactor idea is not new. The engineering test reactor was pioneered by the materials

20
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,000
testing reactor located right next door at the National Reactor Testing Station in the

21
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:49,000
the state of Idaho. The Materials Testing Reactor, known as MTR, has been in operation

22
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:56,000
since 1952. The MTR was the first high flux test reactor built in the United States. It

23
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:03,000
is a thermal or slow neutron reactor with U-235 enriched fuel, water as both coolant

24
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:10,000
and moderator, and beryllium as the reflector. Its basic purpose is to provide data on materials

25
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:15,000
materials performance and characteristics within an environment of intense nuclear radiation.

26
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,000
The experimental facilities consist of spaces within the beryllium reflector

27
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:27,000
and several beamhole penetrations in the shielding and tank extending right up to the core.

28
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:33,000
The materials testing reactor has over 100 experimental positions,

29
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
but was not designed for in-core testing nor for irradiating large samples

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:44,000
size prototype fuel elements intended for power and propulsion reactors. Rapid

31
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:48,000
advances in reactor technology dictated the need for testing on an engineering

32
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:55,000
scale. While the MTR will still continue with work on smaller materials samples

33
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:00,000
and experiments of a research nature, the ETR just a few yards to the south is the

34
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
next step forward in the crucial testing program. The design of the engineering

35
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:12,000
test reactor was derived in part from a conceptual design study prepared by Phillips Petroleum

36
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Company, operators of both the MTR and the ETR. Construction was begun late in 1955.

37
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:25,000
Design and construction had to be accomplished almost simultaneously. The work was done for

38
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:33,000
the Atomic Energy Commission by a single architect-engineer-constructor, Kaiser Engineers. They, in turn, assigned

39
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,000
the nuclear design project to the General Electric Company.

40
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,000
Construction proceeded on schedule, even through sub-zero winter temperatures.

41
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Kaiser engineers used their nationwide system of offices

42
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:48,000
to expedite the manufacture of components at the various subcontractors' locations.

43
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Vigorous liaison between the Atomic Energy Commission,

44
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:57,000
the many contractors, and the Phillips Petroleum Company dovetailed all facets of the big project.

45
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:01,000
And late in December of 1956, the reactor building was ready

46
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,000
to receive the huge reactor pressure vessel.

47
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:10,000
The 150,000 pound pressure vessel was fabricated in Boston, Massachusetts, some 3,000 miles

48
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:15,000
from Idaho and was delivered on special railroad equipment.

49
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:19,000
Shipping difficulties made it necessary to remove some of the exterior equipment, such

50
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:23,000
as nozzles and flanges which were replaced upon arrival.

51
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:28,000
The vessel is made of carbon steel with interior cladding of stainless steel.

52
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:35,000
It is about 35 feet long and 12 feet in diameter at the top, with a lower section diameter of 8 feet.

53
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:42,000
The pressure vessel is supported on a bed plate at the transition level, from the larger to the smaller diameter.

54
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:48,000
This support method provides for thermal expansion of the vessel diametrically without excessive stress or movement.

55
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:59,000
The pressure vessel is designed for 250 psig at 200 degrees Fahrenheit and will operate at 185 psig

56
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,000
All design criteria were established with similar safety factors included

57
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:10,000
The point of highest pressure will occur on the bottom head of the vessel due to the weight of the water column

58
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:16,000
It has a cluster of perforations to accommodate the control rod drives and experimental loops

59
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:24,000
The bottom head will be subject to periodic loads as high as 20,900 PSI under full scram conditions.

60
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:30,000
This assumes an optimum combined weight of more than 180 tons for the grid plate,

61
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:37,000
internal thermal shields, water, core components, experimental facilities, and control rod shock load.

62
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:44,000
In its final position, the vessel protrudes a few feet above the top floor level of the reactor building

63
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,000
and extends downward three stories to the sub-pile room.

64
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,000
Particular panes had to be taken in the fabrication of the grid plate.

65
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:56,000
Since this is the positioning device for the core components,

66
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:02,000
extremely high tolerances were imperative through every phase of manufacture and machining.

67
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Before the basic forging was complete, two blanks had to be rejected.

68
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:12,000
The grid plate is 9 1⁄2 inches thick and 62 1⁄2 inches in diameter.

69
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:19,000
The various perforations will guide the control rods, position the fuel elements, and accommodate the experimental facilities.

70
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:30,000
The close tolerances exercised in the machining of the grid plate provide precise control over the positioning of the core components,

71
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:34,000
plus such structural support that an upper grid plate will not be required.

72
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:43,000
In operation, there will be a combined thermal and mechanical load on the plate of 16,300 PSI.

73
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:50,000
The grid plate is positioned at the bottom of the inner reactor tank midway in the narrow

74
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:57,000
section of the pressure vessel and is supported by stainless steel I-beams 8 feet long. Directly

75
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:03,000
below the grid plate, positioning pads prevent excessive lateral motion. Through the supports,

76
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:07,000
the grid plate load is transmitted to the bottom head of the pressure vessel.

77
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:12,000
The core structure is built upon the grid plate in the following arrangement.

78
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:21,000
There are 49 fuel elements surrounded by a beryllium reflector 4.5 inches thick,

79
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:25,000
an outer reflector of aluminum sections,

80
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,000
plus a permanent aluminum blanket.

81
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:40,000
There are 16 control rods within the fuel section and two regulating rods located in the beryllium reflector.

82
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:46,000
The neutron source is also located in the beryllium reflector.

83
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:53,000
Finally, there are 17 openings in the core and reflector for experimental facilities.

84
00:08:53,000 --> 00:09:01,000
The control rods for the engineering test reactor are unique, not only in positioning, but also in design

85
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:07,000
Four of the 16 control rods are called black poison rods

86
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:12,000
The poison section consisting of boron enriched type 304 stainless steel

87
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:16,000
The 12 remaining rods are called gray poison rods

88
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:20,000
The poison being type A nickel, low in cobalt content

89
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:33,000
Each rod consists of a poison section at the top, a fuel section in the middle, and a shock section at the bottom, assembled end-to-end

90
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:41,000
This assembly is latched to a drive mechanism which moves the fuel and poison sections in and out of the reactor core through a guide tube

91
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:44,000
The shock section does not enter the core

92
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:50,000
In operation, the black poison rods are used in start-up and shut-down

93
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:53,000
while the gray rods are used for operational control

94
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:59,000
In low position, the gray rods will overcome 12% excess reactivity

95
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:04,000
In shut-down, the black rods will handle 14.4% excess

96
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:12,000
The two regulating rods in the reflector are of the black poison type

97
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:18,000
Each capable of overcoming 0.5% excess and are used as the reactor fine control.

98
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:28,000
The fuel elements for the ETR are flat, plate-type, aluminum-boron-uranium assemblies.

99
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:33,000
Each element contains 19 fuel plates, 37 inches long,

100
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:38,000
positioned in the side plates so that there is a coolant passage between the plates.

101
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:46,000
The incorporation of a burnable poison, boron, in the fuel element extends the useful life of the fuel

102
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,000
and provides more uniform flux distribution in the core

103
00:10:55,000 --> 00:11:01,000
Since performance characteristics of the core depend largely upon the contents of the experimental spaces

104
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:06,000
a low power mock-up of the ETR core was set up in an adjacent building

105
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:10,000
We're concerned here only with nuclear parameters,

106
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:13,000
so this low-power core assembly provides us with valuable data

107
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:17,000
which are applied in setting up an experiment in the ETR.

108
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:24,000
Thus, prior to any proposed test, proper core loading, control, and behavior are determined here,

109
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:27,000
saving much actual reactor operating time.

110
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:33,000
Since the control rod drives are located at the bottom of the reactor,

111
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:37,000
all of the space at the top level is free for experimental facilities.

112
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:43,000
In addition, access to the core is simplified. The penetrations of the

113
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:48,000
active part of the core range from 9 by 9 inches to 3 by 3 inches and extend for

114
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:53,000
the length of the core or 36 inches. Facility tubes may be inserted in the

115
00:11:53,000 --> 00:12:00,000
spaces providing for external control of experiments during operations. In a

116
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:04,000
In a typical experiment, for example, of the single-pass air loop type,

117
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:07,000
the test material will be placed in a facility tube,

118
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:10,000
which also permits circulation of air.

119
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:13,000
Radial penetrations of the shielding and vessel

120
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:16,000
could accommodate several such experiments.

121
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,000
Instrument leads will be attached, and in some cases,

122
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:23,000
mechanical linkages for specimen manipulation can be provided.

123
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:26,000
The tube leaves the reactor through the bottom head,

124
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:29,000
where a ninety-degree turn leads it through a port into the basement

125
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:32,000
where it is routed to the stack discharge system.

126
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:37,000
Other experimental loops utilize access sleeves within the outer shielding.

127
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:41,000
These are high-pressure, closed, recirculating water loops.

128
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:47,000
The lines run from bottom floor cubicles through the reactor shield to the sub-pile room,

129
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:52,000
then to the reactor top via access sleeves in the concrete biological shield.

130
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:56,000
The lines enter the reactor vessel through the access nozzles,

131
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:03,000
pass down through the core and out through access holes in the reactor bottom plug into the subpile room.

132
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:11,000
The lines leave the subpile room and re-enter the cubicle through the access sleeves in the subpile room wall.

133
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:19,000
All of the experimental services such as fluid circulation and heat exchange, power supply, and instrumentation

134
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:23,000
are connected externally and don't interfere with reactor operation.

135
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:34,000
Still another category is the package loop in which the circulating pumps and heat exchanger are integral parts of the loop entirely contained within the reactor tank.

136
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:40,000
Access to the loop is through a nozzle in the upper part of the reactor vessel.

137
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:45,000
The reactor also can accommodate a large number of capsule type experiments.

138
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:52,000
In operation, the top of the reactor is shielded by removable concrete sections, assembled

139
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:57,000
in an igloo-like arrangement, completely closing off the top of the reactor.

140
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:02,000
Early in construction, prior to the arrival of the reactor vessel, an external thermal

141
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:04,000
shield was installed.

142
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:09,000
It is made of lead, three and a half inches thick, in which stainless tubing is embedded.

143
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:13,000
Primary system water is circulated through these tubes for cooling.

144
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:18,000
In addition, an internal thermal shield is placed around the core between the inner reactor

145
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:21,000
tank and the pressure vessel walls.

146
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:27,000
The inner shield consists of four concentric steel cylinders, each 57 inches high, separated

147
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
to allow the flow of water.

148
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:35,000
This design feature was dictated by the 175 megawatt power output of the core.

149
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:42,000
The combined stress on the inner shield is 2,200 psi, which is less than 10% of its yield strength.

150
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:49,000
The external and internal thermal shielding surrounds the core area in general

151
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:55,000
and will serve to limit the temperature at the inner face of the concrete biological shield to 120 degrees Fahrenheit.

152
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:02,000
The reactor is cooled by demineralized water flowing at about 44,000 gallons per minute

153
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:05,000
or at 35 feet per second through the core area.

154
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:11,000
It enters the reactor through a 36 inch pipe in the upper vessel.

155
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:15,000
The water is then deflected upward by a flow distributor.

156
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:19,000
The distributor, by changing flow direction, minimizes turbulence and hydraulic loads on

157
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:27,000
internal installations, and acts to dissipate input vortices and vibrations from the pumps.

158
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:32,000
The water reverses and flows down through the core and back up along the internal thermal

159
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:39,000
shield to a 36-inch discharge pipe. These pipes are located in a shielded tunnel that leads to

160
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:44,000
a separate heat exchanger building where the reactor fission heat is transferred to a secondary

161
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:52,000
cooling system, both systems being separate loops. From the secondary loop, the reactor heat is

162
00:15:52,000 --> 00:16:00,000
dissipated through conventional cooling towers. The engineering test reactor operates on a 20-day

163
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:08,000
cycle. In that period, average fuel burnup will be 33%. The absence of top controls greatly

164
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:14,000
simplifies the change-out procedure. Long grapples are used with special grasping hooks

165
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:19,000
to engage the fuel element handle. Once the fuel element is engaged by the grapple, it

166
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:27,000
is passed through a nozzle in the reactor tank and deposited in a transfer chute. This

167
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:34,000
opening is closed during reactor operation. The transfer chute at the reactor end of the

168
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:40,000
canal is hydraulically operated, tilting away from the reactor vessel at an angle that permits

169
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:46,000
easy removal of the spent element. It is grappled from the chute and placed in one of the storage

170
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:53,000
racks in the canal. During startup operations, a high water level in the canal was not necessary.

171
00:16:53,000 --> 00:17:01,000
In normal operations, the canal is filled for maximum shielding since the activity rate of each element exceeds one million roentgens per hour.

172
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:12,000
In the working area of the canal, there is a remote operated saw which allows the operator to cut irradiated material into convenient lengths for storage or handling.

173
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:21,000
The canal is a T-shape with a 60-foot storage canal and a 35-foot working area in the stem of the T.

174
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:30,000
The canal is a uniform 20 feet in depth, except for this well, directly under the transfer chute.

175
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:35,000
The junction of the two canals may be sealed with a removable bulkhead,

176
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:38,000
permitting separate drainage of each section.

177
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:42,000
The storage section is 60 feet long and 12 feet wide,

178
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
allowing ample room for manipulation of equipment.

179
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:50,000
The compressor building adjoins the reactor building

180
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:54,000
and houses air compressors and heaters for experimental facilities.

181
00:17:55,000 --> 00:18:00,000
30 pounds of air per second at 320 PSIG pressure is supplied from here.

182
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:08,000
Also, 24 pounds of this air can be heated and manifolded into a single header for experimenters' use.

183
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:16,000
The ETR-MTR electrical facilities for general power and experimental power have been greatly expanded.

184
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,000
The power requirements for the ETR area alone

185
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:24,000
consist of a connected load of 17,800 kilovolt amperes,

186
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:28,000
of which 8,000 kVA is of test facilities.

187
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:33,000
Formal ceremonies laid in 1957

188
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:37,000
officially dedicated the engineering test reactor to private industry

189
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:40,000
and the vital research necessary to the rapid development

190
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:43,000
of economical and plentiful nuclear energy.

191
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:51,000
The full design core loading was reached September 26th, one week after criticality had been achieved.

192
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:59,000
And the post-neutron test continued for many weeks, during which the reactor's operational behavior was observed at increasing power.

193
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:08,000
Certain difficulties related to the mechanical stability of the first batch of fuel elements became evident during pre-neutron testing.

194
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:14,000
It was found also that the fuel had a higher than design content of the poison boron

195
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:17,000
because of metallurgical and analytical uncertainties,

196
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:20,000
with resultant lower than design excess reactivity.

197
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:28,000
However, solutions to these problems were found, and by early 1958, the reactor was running at approximately 90 megawatts,

198
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:32,000
the full power required for the first major experiment.

199
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:37,000
With this first experiment completed, other experimental loops were installed,

200
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:41,000
and the power was increased as needed up to the design value.

201
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:46,000
This, in essence, is the engineering test reactor.

202
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
Its design, purpose, and scope.

203
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:56,000
The engineering test reactor marks a distinct advance in test reactor capabilities.

204
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:00,000
This film on the design and construction of the ETR

205
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:03,000
was prepared by the United States Atomic Energy Commission

206
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:08,000
to promote understanding of our activities in the development of such facilities

207
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:11,000
and their relation to the nuclear power program.
